Yes, under Washington's Residential Landlord-Tenant Act, if you notify your landlord that you or someone sharing your unit has been threatened with a firearm or other deadly weapon, and the person who made the threat has been arrested, you may have the right to end your lease early without penalty.

Specifically, RCW 59.18.352 says:

- If you notify your landlord that you or another tenant in your unit was threatened with a gun or deadly weapon;
- The person who made the threat is arrested;
- And if the landlord does **not** file an eviction action against the threatening tenant within 7 days of being notified of the arrest;

Then you can terminate the rental agreement by giving written notice to your landlord and leave the premises without any further financial obligation under the lease. You are discharged from paying rent after the day you leave, and you should receive a pro-rated refund of any prepaid rent, plus a detailed explanation of any withheld deposit (RCW 59.18.352).

Note that the landlord is **not required** to evict the threatening tenant, but if they do not act within the 7-day window, you get the option to end your lease without penalty.

In short, if your landlord doesn't act promptly after a serious threat and arrest, you have the legal right to terminate your lease early to protect your safety without financial penalty. 

Keep in mind this is legal information, not legal advice. You may want to consult a lawyer or tenant support organizations for help with your specific situation.
